finding.problem:
The standard models (𝔐_std), card-added witnesses and dim_lb witnesses are confined to the HB families within these ranges, and BP-7 'never enlarges FB or dim_lb'. Meanwhile AI-3 credit instances draw every continuous primitive log-uniformly over [10⁻³, 10³]; DIF-7 can exclude only 10% of draws. θ_dict must carry each input's declared meaning, and the auditor may narrow it.

Credit consequence: on a large share of credit instances θ_dict(𝒞_ι) falls outside the ranges. 𝔐_std(ι|H) is then empty and c_ι = κ_ι = 0. Because b_J takes its minimum over all of I_𝒦, b_J = 0, which fires KU-11 (NON-COMPRESSIVE).

Lock consequence: on a real platform whose BP-5 inputs lie outside the grammar, no in-range witness is 'consistent with every BP-5 input'. CHARTER TEST pattern: a platform with a measured broadband spectral density that ≤ 8 modes in [0.5, 2] cannot carry, or with β·ω > 8. Then:
- Q3(a) has no witness;
- dim_lb is uncertified;
- there is no live rival;
- J_SRB = ∅, which the §15.5 table scores RESTATED, because J_K ⊇ ∅ holds trivially.

This fails every card that has continuous instance inputs, or a platform that is not exactly an HB member.

verdict.fix_problems:
The proposed fix has four problems.

(1) "B-SEL embeddings and SEL-H items … are scored by §15.1 only" is too broad. Read literally, it exempts these instances from every rule outside §15.1, including:
- KU-2: "pNS violated in a Bell embedding" is a universal kill;
- KU-1 and KU-3;
- KU-5 and §15.8, which sit outside §15.1;
- DC-8: the consistency flag, computed "on every B-SEL embedding";
- BU-4(b): realized-set distinctness on AI-4;
- G4-NR: the NR tests re-run on AI-1 to AI-5;
- DIF-1 and DIF-7 on every in-domain instance.

That is a loophole, and it contradicts those clauses. The exception must be from Q1 only.

(2) The leading qualifier "on every in-domain instance on which the frozen chart is defined" makes Q1's reach depend on where the chart happens to be defined. A card could then make the chart undefined (⊥ extractors, discrete records) to dodge Q1. The fix's last sentence pulls the other way, and the two conflict. The exemption should be keyed to which battery an instance comes from, not to whether the chart is defined.

(3) "Every AI-4 embedding whose repertoire is the frozen templates" lets the card's Emb, through its template-to-Ξ map, help decide which instances Q1 covers. It is probably an empty set anyway. An AI-4 embedding that is not a B-SEL item and whose repertoire is not the templates falls both outside the list and under the "any other in-domain instance → Q1 failure" catch-all. The fix contradicts itself there.

(4) Two smaller points:
- The exemption should also cover pool-(a) items that later join the public battery (§15.9, sequential cards).
- It should explicitly never cover AI-1, AI-2, AI-3, AI-5, credit instances or lock fibers. Otherwise a card could make AI-1 a Bell embedding to shrink Q1's reach.

Adding the lock fibers to the list is harmless: the §1.5 robustness clause already implies Q1 there.

finding.problem:
Only item 1 of the adopted checklist has a tolerance; the other items are absolutes. Read literally under CV-1:
- item 9: some nonzero cross-talk exists on every physical platform;
- item 6: a causal detector kernel with an exponential tail has infinite support, so no finite record contains it;
- items 2 and 4: protocol-independent noise and timing are absolutes;
- item 8: on the P-2 counting platforms that MC-11 itself lists, records are integer counts, so 'quantization … excluded unless provably inactive' can never hold.

MC-11 has the Intake auditor pre-assess every item as satisfiable on the platform, otherwise CARD-UNMEASURABLE. So every card fails at S7, and SC3 is unreachable.

finding.fix_wording:
Add to MC-6: "Each Appendix-G item is read at charter resolution:
- an absolute in items 2–9 ('no', 'only', 'protocol-independent', 'full support', 'excluded') holds if the item's effect on every lock observable, propagated through the frozen estimator, is certified below σ_pre/Q_min;
- a kernel's support is the interval outside which its BL-norm weight is below 2^(−p★) of its total;
- a record that is discrete by its declared type (counts, outcomes) is not quantization under item 8.
This is a Stage-3 reading of the adopted checklist, not an edit to R1."

verdict.final_wording:
Add to MC-6 (it also governs the MC-11 pre-assessment):

"**Reading of Appendix G** (a Stage-3 reading of the adopted checklist, not an edit to R1).
- **Magnitude clauses hold when bounded, not only when exactly zero:**
  - residual uncalibrated nonlinearity and protocol dependence of readout noise (item 2);
  - gain and offset variation beyond the calibrated model (item 3);
  - protocol-dependent clock jitter, latency and environment phase (item 4);
  - kernel weight outside the record (item 6);
  - residual saturation, clipping, quantization or other non-invertible nonlinearity (item 8);
  - cross-talk (item 9).
- **How they are decided.** The MC-6 agent certifies an upper bound on each, in apparatus units, from interface-side calibration data only (MC-2), and freezes the bounds with the checklist. Before unsealing, the Evaluator propagates the frozen bounds, summed over all magnitude clauses, through the frozen estimators and charter code. This propagation is part of MC-6 and uses no lock data. The clauses hold only if:
  - (a) the summed shift of each lock observable's estimate, and of J_K through every input the prediction consumes, is at most σ_pre/Q_min, and that shift is included in σ_pre; and
  - (b) the summed bound, propagated to every statistic that decides a carrier, MODE SELECTION or T-tier verdict on the platform, is smaller than that statistic's margin from its decision boundary.
- **Design clauses are read exactly:**
  - the fixed time base, sampling grid, trigger phase and window (item 4);
  - one readout channel with fixed weights (item 5);
  - invertibility on, and injectivity into, the record (item 6);
  - no outcome-dependent selection (item 7);
  - item 10.
- **Discrete records.** A recorded variable that is itself discrete (a count or outcome of S or E) is not quantized under item 8. The map from the detector signal to that variable (discrimination, dead time, pile-up) is a nonlinearity under items 1, 2 and 8, and is bounded as above."

verdict.reasoning:
I tried to refute the defect and could not. Read as a whole, the charter kills an honest SCOPE-G card at this clause.

1. **The clause binds every scope.** The §2.5 "Nontrivial reduction" clause (lines 651-655) has no scope qualifier. SC1 (§3.2) requires it on every lock fiber, and S6 makes it terminal (CARD-UNINFORMATIVE). Q9 shows the drafters knew how to limit ε conditions to scope Q: its sentence "Scope Q additionally: ... ε^{T_Π} > r_lock" separates them. §2.5 does not. No other clause rescues a SCOPE-G card:
   - §14 SCOPE-G, RS-1 to RS-4, MC-1 and §15.5 B-SRB contain no exemption.
   - CV-1 resolves the ambiguity against the card.

2. **Fibers with no positive ε.** STD-3 states that under RH-KMS, RH-GAU and linear coupling, 𝒜_ε = {0}. The RH-GAU row also gives ε^T = 0 for T ⊇ T_caus. The excluded set lies in FB, so on such a fiber it cannot meet {ε > r_lock} for any r_lock ≥ 0. Such fibers are easy to land on:
   - By BP-4, RH-GAU holds unless it is certified to fail by δ_RH. Near-Gaussian large baths (STD-9) therefore count as RH-GAU.
   - §1.5 forces at least one lock fiber to match the platform's regime.
   - MC-11 expressly contemplates RH-KMS platforms, and G2-12 item 4 steers only scope-Q cards away from them.

   A fully linear platform (linear S, Gaussian E) would also fail MC-4, because BP-5 hands the baseline the calibrated linear response and the correlators. But with Gaussian E, linear coupling and a nonlinear system (a spin-boson or Caldeira–Leggett class), higher-order response is only partly fixed under KMS (STD-3). There, an honest SCOPE-G card can plausibly pass MC-4 and still die at S6.

3. **Units mismatch.** Appendix A defines r_lock "per observable". A SCOPE-G card has no ε lock observable, so under CV-1 its r_lock is read in the response functional's own units (window = its range over B-HB). That r_lock can exceed every ε value FB realizes, since ε ≤ 2 and realized values are usually much smaller. The card then fails on any fiber.

4. **The rule's purpose does not reach scope G.** The guard came from the working record's red-team pattern RT-11: stop a relation that restricts only the degenerate ε = 0 locus. That concern belongs to scope Q. Addition 4 created SCOPE-G because ε is blind to linear response, and RS-1 says ε = 0 does not mean no response.

5. **Lifting the condition for scope G opens no loophole.** These guards stay in force:
   - nonempty relative interior in FB, which already blocks a measure-zero ε = 0 locus;
   - "meets the realized-Π region";
   - Q9 for any scope;
   - Q3(a): witnesses in 𝓗 ∖ Z(ℛ★) violating ℛ★ by at least 3·r_lock in a lock observable;
   - the Q4 W-pipe;
   - SCOPE-G's own requirement that FB admit a violation with the linear part of Γ held fixed;
   - RS-4, which moves an ε-only relation back to SCOPE-Q and so back under the guard.

**Severity: MAJOR, not BLOCKER.** Scope G still survives on non-Gaussian, non-KMS fibers whose r_lock is small. But once a 𝒦 draft is logged (§19.2), this kill could never be loosened.

finding.problem:
HB-5's role is 'T-dependence of the zero set'. In R1's record, C2-F is > 0 under E₂± and is 0 only 'once the calibrated filters are added to T' (T_lin or T_caus). The WO-001 C2 report reads the nonzero value as 'the correct value for each class, not a false positive'.

Requiring exactly 0 at T_Π itself therefore fails every generated T_Π that does not contain the protocol-dependent time-mixing filter maps. That includes a T_Π equal to R1's own Tier-2 class T_mono, and any coordinatewise class ⊇ E₂± that SCOPE-Q allows. The consequences:
- T_Π is not GENERATED (DIF-5(e));
- every ε^{T_Π} relation fails Q8 at S5;
- G4-DIF fails.
CHARTER TEST pattern: a card whose generated T_Π is a coordinatewise class.

Transplant draws are B-HB models with 'generic parameters under the reference measure'. The frozen coupling range [0, 1] includes decoupling.

CHARTER TEST pattern: a relation homogeneous in response, whose two sides vanish together at S–E decoupling. Such a relation 'holds within r_lock' on every weakly coupled draw. Computed, for observables scaling as gᵠ with r_lock/scale = r:
- r = 0.02, q = 2: 14% of uniform draws;
- r = 0.05, q = 3: 37% of uniform draws;
- 67–86% if the range is drawn log-uniform on [10⁻³, 1].

Each case exceeds the 10% threshold, so Q3(c) returns CARD-STANDARD for every honest card in that class. G2-12 item 5 fixes this pathology only in NR-4, which itself borrows 'the transplant threshold of Q3(c)'. Q3(c) still kills.

R_card = 5000 core-hours caps the card's declared per-item cost multiplied by an item count the Evaluator controls. That count includes:
- AI-3 draws plus redraws up to 10·N_cred = 1000 attempts, at r★ and r★★;
- 100 credit instances with dimension certificates;
- B-SEL, where SEL-1 alone has 1721 tables, each run with ≥ 2 Emb seeds and at DC-4 levels n, n+1 and 2n;
- NR-4, NR-6 and NR-7 runs, Q3(b) and Q3(c) draws, and the MC-7 simulations.

This is several thousand runs, so the average falls below about 1 core-hour, and the card cannot know the count at freeze. Under CV-1 the overrun becomes CARD-UNDECIDABLE, or UNSCORABLE, which is never reopened.

T_audit = 60 days runs from the card's freeze and must cover S3–S8 plus S9. If S3–S8 are slow (100 dimension certificates, each witness refereed by a skeptic), S9 incompleteness can be attributed to the author side → CARD-RESTATED.
